1
00:00:02,600 --> 00:00:10,403
Every once in a while, a small team comes along that changes how a big organization makes decisions.

2
00:00:10,803 --> 00:00:12,647
Not with more meetings.

3
00:00:12,667 --> 00:00:14,689
Not with more slides.

4
00:00:14,889 --> 00:00:16,441
With evidence.

5
00:00:17,141 --> 00:00:20,933
Today, I want to show you exactly how that team works.

6
00:00:21,133 --> 00:00:22,586
Four people.

7
00:00:22,606 --> 00:00:23,910
One loop.

8
00:00:23,930 --> 00:00:27,146
And a completely different way to build products.

9
00:00:27,546 --> 00:00:31,594
We call it the Ninja Team Product Operating System.

10
00:00:32,194 --> 00:00:33,746
Let's begin.

11
00:00:35,396 --> 00:00:37,588
So, what is a ninja team?

12
00:00:37,738 --> 00:00:43,685
It's a four-person strike team. Small. High-agency. And built for one job.

13
00:00:43,735 --> 00:00:47,080
Turning unclear opportunities into three things.

14
00:00:47,100 --> 00:00:48,823
Validated signals.

15
00:00:48,843 --> 00:00:50,353
Customer pull.

16
00:00:50,373 --> 00:00:52,437
And product decisions.

17
00:00:52,537 --> 00:00:55,113
With low effort, and high value.

18
00:00:55,613 --> 00:00:58,488
Now, here's the part most people miss.

19
00:00:58,688 --> 00:01:04,272
The goal of the ninja team is not to ship the final enterprise platform first.

20
00:01:04,572 --> 00:01:06,999
The goal is to create evidence.

21
00:01:07,599 --> 00:01:10,239
Remember that word. Evidence.

22
00:01:10,339 --> 00:01:13,683
Everything else in this system flows from it.

23
00:01:15,533 --> 00:01:20,605
Let's meet the team. Four roles. And every single one of them matters.

24
00:01:20,755 --> 00:01:22,848
First, the product manager.

25
00:01:22,868 --> 00:01:26,995
The PM defines the problem, and what success looks like.

26
00:01:27,015 --> 00:01:29,051
Chooses the wedge use case.

27
00:01:29,071 --> 00:01:31,768
Runs priorities and customer learning.

28
00:01:31,788 --> 00:01:35,239
And owns the decision rhythm, and the narrative.

29
00:01:35,439 --> 00:01:37,681
Second, the solution architect.

30
00:01:37,701 --> 00:01:40,249
Shapes the end-to-end system concept.

31
00:01:40,269 --> 00:01:42,881
Reduces technical risk, early.

32
00:01:42,901 --> 00:01:47,049
Finds what can be reused, versus what must be invented.

33
00:01:47,069 --> 00:01:51,331
And translates the demo into an architecture that can scale.

34
00:01:51,531 --> 00:01:53,859
Third, the front-end developer.

35
00:01:53,879 --> 00:01:56,939
Builds a visible user experience, fast.

36
00:01:56,959 --> 00:02:00,040
Makes the demo feel real, and intuitive.

37
00:02:00,060 --> 00:02:02,821
Turns concepts into clickable proof.

38
00:02:02,841 --> 00:02:06,825
And captures workflow friction, the moment it shows up.

39
00:02:07,025 --> 00:02:09,609
And fourth, the back-end developer.

40
00:02:09,629 --> 00:02:12,817
Builds the core logic, and the integrations.

41
00:02:12,837 --> 00:02:16,516
Creates realistic data, APIs, and rules.

42
00:02:16,536 --> 00:02:18,935
Makes workflow outcomes measurable.

43
00:02:18,955 --> 00:02:21,872
And stress-tests feasibility, early.

44
00:02:22,072 --> 00:02:25,044
Direction. Architecture. Experience. Logic.

45
00:02:25,064 --> 00:02:31,240
Put those four people together, and you have a complete product team, in miniature.

46
00:02:31,740 --> 00:02:35,127
Now, I want to be really clear about one thing.

47
00:02:35,227 --> 00:02:38,956
The product manager is not a project secretary.

48
00:02:38,976 --> 00:02:40,742
Not the note-taker.

49
00:02:40,762 --> 00:02:43,594
Not the person chasing status updates.

50
00:02:43,894 --> 00:02:46,656
The PM is the product captain.

51
00:02:46,676 --> 00:02:48,883
The decision integrator.

52
00:02:48,903 --> 00:02:53,847
The one who takes everything the team learns, and turns it into a decision.

53
00:02:55,697 --> 00:03:00,535
So how does this team think? It comes down to five first principles.

54
00:03:00,685 --> 00:03:03,845
One. Speed to signal beats speed to scale.

55
00:03:03,865 --> 00:03:09,449
Before you build for a million users, find out if one user actually cares.

56
00:03:09,749 --> 00:03:12,311
Two. Demo before bureaucracy.

57
00:03:12,331 --> 00:03:18,107
A working demo can answer in ten minutes what a document debates for ten weeks.

58
00:03:18,407 --> 00:03:21,781
Three. Solve one painful journey, end to end.

59
00:03:21,801 --> 00:03:26,318
Not ten features, half done. One journey, done completely.

60
00:03:26,618 --> 00:03:29,117
Four. Learning beats assumptions.

61
00:03:29,137 --> 00:03:34,785
Every opinion in the room is just a hypothesis, until a customer proves it.

62
00:03:35,085 --> 00:03:37,711
And five. Reuse before rebuild.

63
00:03:37,731 --> 00:03:43,273
If something already works, use it. Spend your energy only where it's truly new.

64
00:03:43,673 --> 00:03:47,226
Five principles. Simple to say. Hard to live.

65
00:03:47,246 --> 00:03:51,219
Great ninja teams live them every single day.

66
00:03:53,069 --> 00:03:56,233
Now, a ninja team doesn't live in a vacuum.

67
00:03:56,253 --> 00:04:02,429
It has to plug into the organization you already have. Here's how. Five steps.

68
00:04:02,629 --> 00:04:04,673
Step one. Find the wedge.

69
00:04:04,693 --> 00:04:08,012
Pick one high-pain, high-visibility workflow.

70
00:04:08,032 --> 00:04:13,626
Where customer frustration is obvious, and success is easy to observe.

71
00:04:13,826 --> 00:04:16,467
Step two. Define the hypothesis.

72
00:04:16,487 --> 00:04:20,237
If we show this outcome clearly, the customer will care.

73
00:04:20,257 --> 00:04:25,354
They'll lean in, give us their attention, or ask for a next-step discussion.

74
00:04:25,374 --> 00:04:28,535
Write it down, before you build anything.

75
00:04:28,735 --> 00:04:31,759
Step three. Build a thin-slice POC.

76
00:04:31,779 --> 00:04:37,837
Only the minimum workflow, data logic, and interface needed to prove customer value.

77
00:04:37,857 --> 00:04:39,198
Nothing more.

78
00:04:39,398 --> 00:04:41,569
Step four. Demo with intent.

79
00:04:41,589 --> 00:04:43,410
You're not there to impress.

80
00:04:43,430 --> 00:04:48,432
You're there to test appetite, objections, trust, and operational fit.

81
00:04:48,632 --> 00:04:52,766
And step five. Convert the signal into product action.

82
00:04:52,786 --> 00:04:55,981
Decide. Stop, refine, pilot, or productize.

83
00:04:56,001 --> 00:05:00,887
Then feed the results into the roadmap, and into governance.

84
00:05:01,287 --> 00:05:04,418
Find. Define. Build. Demo. Convert.

85
00:05:04,468 --> 00:05:08,153
That's how a small team earns a big seat at the table.

86
00:05:10,003 --> 00:05:14,137
Which brings us to the heart of the whole system. The loop.

87
00:05:14,437 --> 00:05:20,682
It starts with customer pain. Real pain. Not a technology looking for a problem.

88
00:05:20,732 --> 00:05:23,181
The pain becomes a hypothesis.

89
00:05:23,201 --> 00:05:25,820
The hypothesis becomes a POC.

90
00:05:25,840 --> 00:05:28,118
The POC becomes a demo.

91
00:05:28,138 --> 00:05:30,935
The demo produces a customer reaction.

92
00:05:30,955 --> 00:05:33,617
And that reaction drives a decision.

93
00:05:33,817 --> 00:05:37,027
And there are only four possible decisions.

94
00:05:37,047 --> 00:05:38,101
Kill it.

95
00:05:38,121 --> 00:05:39,389
Refine it.

96
00:05:39,409 --> 00:05:40,592
Pilot it.

97
00:05:40,612 --> 00:05:42,548
Or productize it.

98
00:05:42,848 --> 00:05:46,496
And hear me on this. Killing an idea is not failure.

99
00:05:46,516 --> 00:05:51,995
Killing the wrong idea in two weeks, instead of two years? That's a win.

100
00:05:52,495 --> 00:05:55,185
Along the way, the loop tests three things.

101
00:05:55,205 --> 00:05:56,495
Appetite.

102
00:05:56,515 --> 00:05:57,847
Feasibility.

103
00:05:57,867 --> 00:05:59,696
And business value.

104
00:06:00,096 --> 00:06:04,784
And every demo, every single one, must answer three questions.

105
00:06:04,934 --> 00:06:06,955
Is the problem real?

106
00:06:07,105 --> 00:06:09,020
Is the value clear?

107
00:06:09,170 --> 00:06:11,533
Is the solution believable?

108
00:06:11,933 --> 00:06:16,898
If you can't answer all three with a confident yes, you're not done.

109
00:06:18,748 --> 00:06:22,967
So how do you decide what to work on? You use a filter.

110
00:06:23,017 --> 00:06:27,295
Picture two axes. Effort and complexity, along the bottom.

111
00:06:27,315 --> 00:06:30,606
Value and learning signal, up the side.

112
00:06:30,806 --> 00:06:35,944
Down here, low value, low effort. A perfect UI with no value proof.

113
00:06:35,964 --> 00:06:40,059
It looks beautiful. It proves nothing. Avoid it.

114
00:06:40,259 --> 00:06:45,180
Over here, high effort, low value. Architecture theatre. Big diagrams.

115
00:06:45,200 --> 00:06:48,211
Heavy lifting. No learning. Avoid it.

116
00:06:48,411 --> 00:06:53,378
Up here, high value, but high effort. Heavy platform work. It matters.

117
00:06:53,398 --> 00:06:57,003
But not before you have signal. So, delay it.

118
00:06:57,303 --> 00:07:01,693
And this. This is the prime zone. High value. Low effort.

119
00:07:01,843 --> 00:07:05,031
A simulated, end-to-end user journey.

120
00:07:05,051 --> 00:07:09,092
A clickable front end, with realistic back-end rules.

121
00:07:09,112 --> 00:07:12,705
One key integration, mocked, or partially real.

122
00:07:12,725 --> 00:07:15,551
An operational dashboard prototype.

123
00:07:15,571 --> 00:07:17,976
And a role-based workflow demo.

124
00:07:18,176 --> 00:07:23,312
This is where ninja teams live. Maximum learning. Minimum effort.

125
00:07:25,162 --> 00:07:30,043
So, you've built your thin slice. What does a great demo actually prove?

126
00:07:30,063 --> 00:07:31,279
Five things.

127
00:07:31,379 --> 00:07:34,703
One. The user's pain is real, and urgent.

128
00:07:34,723 --> 00:07:40,394
Two. The outcome is visible in minutes. Not after forty slides of preamble.

129
00:07:40,414 --> 00:07:43,757
Three. The workflow feels operationally credible.

130
00:07:43,777 --> 00:07:47,109
Like it could run on a real Monday morning.

131
00:07:47,129 --> 00:07:52,758
Four. Stakeholders can imagine deploying it. They start asking: who would use this?

132
00:07:52,778 --> 00:07:54,655
Where does it plug in?

133
00:07:54,675 --> 00:07:57,181
And five. The next step is obvious.

134
00:07:57,201 --> 00:08:01,859
Everyone leaves the room knowing exactly what happens next.

135
00:08:02,159 --> 00:08:06,503
And in enterprise and government settings, the bar is higher.

136
00:08:06,523 --> 00:08:09,572
Your demo must also prove four things.

137
00:08:09,592 --> 00:08:10,533
Trust.

138
00:08:10,553 --> 00:08:12,261
Integration fit.

139
00:08:12,281 --> 00:08:14,438
Operational practicality.

140
00:08:14,458 --> 00:08:16,479
And policy awareness.

141
00:08:16,779 --> 00:08:19,910
Wow is nice. But wow is not enough.

142
00:08:19,960 --> 00:08:21,832
Credible beats cool.

143
00:08:23,682 --> 00:08:28,414
Now, let's talk about you. If you're running this team, here is your playbook.

144
00:08:28,434 --> 00:08:29,565
Seven moves.

145
00:08:29,715 --> 00:08:34,488
One. Start from customer pain, not from available technology.

146
00:08:34,538 --> 00:08:39,887
Two. Frame a sharp wedge use case. Narrow beats broad. Every time.

147
00:08:39,937 --> 00:08:45,436
Three. Drive a weekly decision rhythm. Hypothesis. Build. Demo. Learn.

148
00:08:45,486 --> 00:08:51,561
Four. Keep scope ruthless. Always ask: what is the smallest believable proof?

149
00:08:51,611 --> 00:08:57,771
Five. Translate every reaction into product insight. Not meeting notes. Insight.

150
00:08:57,821 --> 00:09:03,938
Six. Protect the team's velocity from random stakeholder noise. You are the shield.

151
00:09:03,988 --> 00:09:10,852
And seven. Turn validated signals into real action. Roadmap. Pilot. Governance.

152
00:09:11,252 --> 00:09:15,407
And if you remember only one sentence, make it this one.

153
00:09:15,707 --> 00:09:19,755
Your job is to maximize learning per unit of effort.

154
00:09:21,805 --> 00:09:25,554
Let me show you what that weekly rhythm can look like.

155
00:09:25,604 --> 00:09:29,199
Monday. Hypothesis. The team agrees on one sentence.

156
00:09:29,219 --> 00:09:33,791
What are we trying to prove this week, and how will we know?

157
00:09:33,891 --> 00:09:38,554
Tuesday through Thursday. Build. The thinnest slice that can prove it.

158
00:09:38,574 --> 00:09:42,653
Front end, back end, and architecture, moving together.

159
00:09:42,753 --> 00:09:47,082
Friday. Demo. Put it in front of real users. Watch their faces.

160
00:09:47,102 --> 00:09:49,297
Listen to their questions.

161
00:09:49,397 --> 00:09:53,769
And then, learn, and decide. Capture the signal. Make the call.

162
00:09:53,789 --> 00:09:57,605
Next Monday, the loop starts again. Only sharper.

163
00:09:57,905 --> 00:10:01,911
Every week, a decision. Not every quarter. Every week.

164
00:10:03,761 --> 00:10:06,721
Let's make this real, with an example.

165
00:10:06,821 --> 00:10:10,151
Field maintenance crews log defects on paper.

166
00:10:10,171 --> 00:10:15,289
Supervisors wait days to approve work orders. Everyone is frustrated.

167
00:10:15,309 --> 00:10:18,911
And everyone can see it. That's your wedge.

168
00:10:19,011 --> 00:10:20,070
The hypothesis.

169
00:10:20,090 --> 00:10:27,539
If supervisors can see live defects, and approve a work order in one tap, they'll ask for a pilot.

170
00:10:27,639 --> 00:10:32,843
The thin slice. One role-based workflow. Realistic, simulated data.

171
00:10:32,863 --> 00:10:36,615
The maintenance system integration? Mocked.

172
00:10:36,715 --> 00:10:41,476
The demo. Ten minutes. A technician logs a defect on a tablet.

173
00:10:41,496 --> 00:10:46,907
A supervisor approves it on a phone. The dashboard updates. Live.

174
00:10:47,007 --> 00:10:51,141
The reaction. "Can we try this with one crew, next month?"

175
00:10:51,241 --> 00:10:53,262
The decision. Pilot.

176
00:10:53,662 --> 00:10:56,736
Two weeks. Four people. One clear decision.

177
00:10:56,756 --> 00:11:00,825
That's the ninja team, working exactly as designed.

178
00:11:02,675 --> 00:11:07,107
Now, how should you lead? Learn from three very different leaders.

179
00:11:07,207 --> 00:11:08,909
From Steve Jobs.

180
00:11:08,929 --> 00:11:11,469
Obsess over the user experience.

181
00:11:11,489 --> 00:11:13,347
Make the story simple.

182
00:11:13,367 --> 00:11:14,991
Say no to noise.

183
00:11:15,011 --> 00:11:18,035
And demo the future, not the features.

184
00:11:18,235 --> 00:11:19,979
From Jensen Huang.

185
00:11:19,999 --> 00:11:22,369
Think full-stack, not in silos.

186
00:11:22,389 --> 00:11:25,122
Master the details that change outcomes.

187
00:11:25,142 --> 00:11:27,320
Tell a clear platform narrative.

188
00:11:27,340 --> 00:11:30,257
And relentlessly compound capability.

189
00:11:30,457 --> 00:11:32,074
From Elon Musk.

190
00:11:32,094 --> 00:11:34,101
Reason from first principles.

191
00:11:34,121 --> 00:11:36,321
Remove unnecessary steps.

192
00:11:36,341 --> 00:11:38,903
Iterate faster than competitors think.

193
00:11:38,923 --> 00:11:42,352
And push for useful truth, from real tests.

194
00:11:42,652 --> 00:11:46,167
Your synthesis? Be the calm product captain.

195
00:11:46,217 --> 00:11:50,481
Customer-obsessed, like Jobs. System-literate, like Jensen.

196
00:11:50,501 --> 00:11:54,169
First-principles and speed-driven, like Elon.

197
00:11:54,219 --> 00:12:01,061
But grounded in enterprise reality, cross-functional alignment, and disciplined product governance.

198
00:12:02,911 --> 00:12:07,898
So how do you know it's working? You measure. But you measure the right things.

199
00:12:07,998 --> 00:12:09,792
Time to first demo.

200
00:12:09,812 --> 00:12:11,777
Customer demos per month.

201
00:12:11,797 --> 00:12:13,847
Customer appetite score.

202
00:12:13,867 --> 00:12:15,861
Pilot conversion rate.

203
00:12:15,881 --> 00:12:17,739
Validated use cases.

204
00:12:17,759 --> 00:12:19,958
Reusable components created.

205
00:12:19,978 --> 00:12:22,348
Roadmap decisions accelerated.

206
00:12:22,368 --> 00:12:25,968
And the ratio of low-effort to high-value work.

207
00:12:26,268 --> 00:12:29,051
Now, notice what's not on this list.

208
00:12:29,071 --> 00:12:30,553
Hours worked.

209
00:12:30,573 --> 00:12:31,990
Tickets closed.

210
00:12:32,010 --> 00:12:33,818
Slides produced.

211
00:12:34,018 --> 00:12:37,810
Measure signal quality. Not activity volume.

212
00:12:39,660 --> 00:12:42,087
So, let's bring it all together.

213
00:12:42,237 --> 00:12:43,690
Four people.

214
00:12:43,710 --> 00:12:45,419
Five principles.

215
00:12:45,439 --> 00:12:46,743
One loop.

216
00:12:46,763 --> 00:12:48,430
Four decisions.

217
00:12:48,450 --> 00:12:51,503
Three questions every demo must answer.

218
00:12:51,523 --> 00:12:53,843
And seven moves to run it all.

219
00:12:55,593 --> 00:12:58,681
A ninja team is a leverage engine.

220
00:12:58,831 --> 00:13:03,583
It doesn't replace the full product organization. It de-risks it.

221
00:13:03,783 --> 00:13:07,505
It uses POCs and demos to discover demand.

222
00:13:07,525 --> 00:13:09,113
Validate value.

223
00:13:09,133 --> 00:13:11,105
Reveal technical truth.

224
00:13:11,125 --> 00:13:13,871
And create momentum for the roadmap.

225
00:13:14,471 --> 00:13:16,663
But... there's one more thing.

226
00:13:17,563 --> 00:13:23,254
The ninja team's real product isn't the POC. It isn't even the demo.

227
00:13:23,454 --> 00:13:25,241
It's the decision.

228
00:13:26,041 --> 00:13:27,914
Move from ideas.

229
00:13:27,934 --> 00:13:29,402
To evidence.

230
00:13:29,422 --> 00:13:31,507
To product decisions.

231
00:13:32,407 --> 00:13:34,493
Now, go find your wedge.

